    "text2": "A team of hydrologists studying a different section of the basin found that rainfall declines were far less severe in areas where clearing occurred in small, scattered patches rather than large contiguous blocks, even when the total percentage of forest lost was similar. They suggest that scattered clearing leaves enough intact forest nearby to sustain much of the region's transpiration and cloud formation, meaning that the pattern of deforestation, not just its overall extent, may determine how much rainfall is actually lost.",
