    "text1": "A twelve-year study following more than eight thousand hospital employees found that nurses who worked rotating night shifts for at least five years had a significantly higher incidence of cardiovascular disease than nurses who worked consistent daytime schedules. The researchers controlled for diet, exercise, smoking, and family history, and still found the association held. They proposed that repeated disruption of the body's circadian rhythm, which governs blood pressure regulation and hormone release, is the most likely mechanism connecting night work to heart disease, though they noted that their study could not establish causation directly.",
    "text2": "A smaller trial at a different hospital system assigned night-shift nurses to a strict light-exposure protocol, using bright light during shifts and blackout curtains during daytime sleep, designed to stabilize their circadian rhythms. After eighteen months, nurses following the protocol showed blood pressure readings similar to daytime workers, while a comparison group of night-shift nurses without the protocol showed the expected elevation, suggesting that the cardiovascular risk of night work may be substantially reduced through circadian-focused interventions rather than being an unavoidable consequence of the schedule itself.",
